By **"the hand-authored `.pathland-*` CSS"** I mean the component-rule block that today lives in `css.rs` — the selectors the renderer puts on its emitted elements:

- `.pathland-button`, `.pathland-input`, `.pathland-toggle`, `.pathland-slider`
- `.pathland-progress`, `.pathland-gauge`, `.pathland-stepper`, `.pathland-menu`
- `.pathland-spinner`, `.pathland-video`, `.pathland-media`, `.pathland-icon`, …

**"Consumes"** means those rules read the design tokens through CSS custom properties instead of hard-coded values. Example from the current `css.rs:179`:

```css
.pathland-button {
  background-color: var(--pl-button-background);
  color: var(--pl-button-foreground);
  border-radius: var(--pl-button-radius);
  padding: var(--pl-button-padding-vertical) var(--pl-button-padding-horizontal);
}
.pathland-button:hover { background-color: var(--pl-button-background-hover); }

.pathland-toggle input[role='switch'] { background-color: var(--pl-toggle-track-off); }
.pathland-toggle input[role='switch']:checked { background-color: var(--pl-toggle-track-on); }
```

The SD **component token layer** supplies `--pl-button-background`, `--pl-toggle-track-on`, etc.; the `.pathland-*` rules consume them.

Why these rules stay hand-authored rather than generated by SD:

- They encode **behavior and structure** — `:hover`, `:focus-visible`, `:disabled`, `::after` switch thumbs, `::-webkit-slider-thumb`, progress pseudo-elements, transitions, menu popovers — not just token values.
- They are tied to the exact DOM the renderer emits (`.pathland-toggle input[role='switch']`, `.pathland-menu-items`, `.pathland-progress::-webkit-progress-value`).
- SD is a token pipeline; generating selectors and state rules from token values is brittle.

So the split is:

| Thing | Source | Example |
|---|---|---|
| **Component tokens** (SD component layer) | Style Dictionary | `component.button.background` → `--pl-button-background` |
| **Component CSS** (hand-authored) | `design/html/components.css` | `.pathland-button { background: var(--pl-button-background) }` |
| **Per-node inline styles** | Protocol opcodes | `style="padding:8px;color:#111"` |

If your goal is that even the component rules are SD-driven, the pragmatic middle ground is to tokenize every remaining hard-coded value so the CSS is almost entirely `var(...)` and only selectors/states remain. Full CSS-rule generation is possible via custom formats but couples token names to the renderer's DOM contract.

